================================================================================
PROGRESS TRACKING
================================================================================

The project uses four reference artifacts:


1. SHORT ROADMAP

    modules
    stages
    progress bars
    percentages
    current position


2. WHOLE ROADMAP

    complete roadmap
    phases
    stages
    progress
    completed topics
    current position


3. CONSPECT

    detailed technical notes
    commands
    concepts
    configurations
    troubleshooting conclusions
    important Q&A


4. CONTEXT

    this document

    describes:
        architecture
        purpose
        learning method
        roadmap
        current state
        design principles
        TODO


All roadmap and conspect documents should use:


    ASCII structure
    code blocks
    tree layouts
    progress bars
    clear separators
    Vim-friendly searchable terminology


================================================================================
DEFINITION OF DONE
================================================================================

The project is considered complete when the student can independently:


    - explain the architecture
    - create and manage the VMs
    - explain the libvirt networking model
    - add a VM to the existing lab network
    - explain the network topology
    - administer Active Directory
    - create / modify / disable users
    - manage groups and permissions
    - configure and troubleshoot DNS
    - configure and troubleshoot DHCP
    - domain-join Windows clients
    - administer Windows 10/11
    - troubleshoot common endpoint issues
    - investigate Event Logs
    - provide remote support
    - manage ITSM tickets
    - manage assets
    - manage software licenses
    - explain ITSM workflows
    - explain cloud IAM
    - deploy cloud resources in Floci
    - use S3 / SQS / Lambda / RDS where supported
    - manage secrets
    - use Terraform
    - understand Terraform state and drift
    - automate repetitive tasks
    - use GitHub Actions
    - troubleshoot failures
    - apply least privilege
    - document infrastructure
    - write useful runbooks
    - write incident reports
    - explain design decisions and alternatives


Most importantly:


    WHAT exists
        +
    WHY it exists
        +
    HOW it works
        +
    HOW to administer it
        +
    HOW to troubleshoot it
        +
    WHAT alternatives exist
        +
    WHAT trade-offs were made


================================================================================
FINAL PORTFOLIO STORY
================================================================================

The final project should tell one coherent story:


    "I built and administered a small engineering company's
     internal IT environment and a local cloud infrastructure
     laboratory."


The core story is:


    Virtualization
        ↓
    libvirt Networking
        ↓
    Local Cloud Laboratory
        ↓
    Cloud Application
        ↓
    Terraform
        ↓
    Windows Server
        ↓
    Active Directory
        ↓
    Windows Client
        ↓
    File Services
        ↓
    IT Support
        ↓
    ITSM
        ↓
    Architecture Integration
        ↓
    TODO / Future Infrastructure


Automation, CI/CD, security and monitoring are
cross-cutting capabilities.

They are not required to become separate mandatory
phases before the core architecture is considered complete.


The student should be able to use this project during
an interview as evidence of practical learning.


================================================================================
PHASE 0 — IMMEDIATE OBJECTIVES
================================================================================

Before implementing the actual company infrastructure:


    1. Create project repository.

    2. Create initial repository structure.

    3. Create README.

    4. Create .gitignore.

    5. Create documentation structure.

    6. Create project TODO.

    7. Document approved architecture.

    8. Prepare libvirt / virt-manager.

    9. Create engineering-lab network.

    10. Verify VM-to-VM networking.

    11. Prepare MINT01.

    12. Prepare DC01 installation media.

    13. Prepare WIN01 installation media.

    14. Establish snapshots / rollback strategy.

    15. Document the initial lab foundation.

    16. Commit the foundation to Git.


Only after this should the project move into:


    PHASE 1 — NETWORKING


================================================================================
IMPORTANT DESIGN PRINCIPLES
================================================================================

1. REALISM OVER COMPLEXITY

Do not add infrastructure simply because it looks impressive.


2. UNDERSTANDING OVER COMMAND MEMORIZATION

The student should understand why a command/configuration exists.


3. SCENARIOS OVER ISOLATED TECHNOLOGIES

Every major technology should have a practical reason to exist.


4. TROUBLESHOOTING IS A FIRST-CLASS SKILL

A working system is only half of the learning.


5. SECURITY FROM THE BEGINNING

Use least privilege and secure defaults whenever practical.


6. DOCUMENT EVERYTHING IMPORTANT

Infrastructure without documentation is incomplete.


7. AUTOMATE AFTER UNDERSTANDING

First perform a task manually.

Then automate it.


8. KEEP THE LAB REPRODUCIBLE

Prefer documented and reproducible configuration.


9. AVOID UNNECESSARY COST

Use local technologies and free/test environments whenever possible.


10. DO NOT OVERBUILD THE VM ENVIRONMENT

Create a new VM only when it has a real purpose.


11. DESIGN NETWORKING FOR GROWTH

The base libvirt network should support additional VMs
without requiring a redesign.


12. BUILD INFRASTRUCTURE BEFORE DEPENDENT SERVICES

Establish:

    virtualization
        ↓
    networking
        ↓
    cloud / infrastructure model
        ↓
    Windows infrastructure
        ↓
    dependent services


Do not introduce higher-level services before
their underlying infrastructure is understood.


13. KEEP THE MASTER CONTEXT STABLE

Detailed implementation should live in:

    tutorials
    conspect
    procedures
    incident reports
    repository documentation


The master context should describe:

    architecture
    goals
    roadmap
    current state
    design decisions
    TODO


14. CONNECT TECHNOLOGIES

The goal is to understand how:


    User
      ↓
    Endpoint
      ↓
    Network
      ↓
    Identity
      ↓
    Permissions
      ↓
    Application
      ↓
    ITSM
      ↓
    Cloud
      ↓
    Infrastructure as Code
      ↓
    Automation
      ↓
    Security
      ↓
    Troubleshooting


work together.


================================================================================
HOW TO CONTINUE TEACHING
================================================================================

When beginning a new phase:


    1. Explain the goal.
    2. Explain the architecture.
    3. Explain the mental model.
    4. Explain the important concepts.
    5. Explain why each component exists.
    6. Explain realistic alternatives.
    7. Explain failure scenarios.
    8. Ask conceptual questions.
    9. Let the student answer.
    10. Discuss / correct the answer.
    11. Let the student implement.
    12. Review the implementation.
    13. Introduce a troubleshooting scenario.
    14. Solve the scenario.
    15. Document the result.
    16. Update the roadmap.
    17. Update project progress.
    18. Continue to the next stage.


Do NOT rush through phases.

Do NOT provide complete configurations immediately
when the goal is to learn them.

Prefer student implementation followed by review.


If the student asks about a previously covered concept:


    Do not restart the entire phase.


Instead:


    answer the specific question
        ↓
    connect it to the existing mental model
        ↓
    continue from the current position


The student prefers a static learning flow:


    CURRENT STAGE
        ↓
    EXPLANATION
        ↓
    DISCUSSION
        ↓
    IMPLEMENTATION
        ↓
    TROUBLESHOOTING
        ↓
    DOCUMENTATION
        ↓
    REVIEW
        ↓
    NEXT STAGE


